WEBVTT

NOTE Saying it out loud

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
In February 1961, the Joint Chiefs of Staff reviewed a CIA plan to invade Cuba at

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.867
the Bay of Pigs. Their report came down to one phrase.

3
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:15.038
The report listed the plan's weak points and then closed with a judgment.

4
00:00:15.038 --> 00:00:18.733
The plan, it said, "has a fair chance of ultimate success."

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:24.405
The officer who drafted that line later said the Chiefs put the odds at roughly

6
00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:25.256
three in ten.

7
00:00:25.256 --> 00:00:30.167
He meant "fair" as a warning, the way a C on a school test means fair work.

8
00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.290
Other readers heard approval.

9
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.601
The commandant of the Marine Corps said the plan should have accomplished the

10
00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:40.800
mission. The CIA quoted the phrase in a paper that argued for the invasion.

11
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.823
President Kennedy came to believe that the Joint Chiefs had endorsed the plan.

12
00:00:45.823 --> 00:00:49.767
After the invasion collapsed, he wondered why no one had warned him.

13
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:54.515
The words were true to the writer, and they failed at the other end.

14
00:00:54.515 --> 00:00:59.334
Uncertainty is not communicated until the listener holds the number the speaker

15
00:00:59.334 --> 00:00:59.700
meant.

16
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.242
Ten years earlier, the same fault had shown up at the CIA.

17
00:01:04.242 --> 00:01:09.542
In March 1951, a National Intelligence Estimate, a formal forecast agreed by the

18
00:01:09.542 --> 00:01:14.909
intelligence agencies, said a Soviet attack on Yugoslavia "should be considered a

19
00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:16.300
serious possibility."

20
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.515
A few days later, the head of the State Department's planning staff asked Sherman

21
00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:23.029
Kent what odds that meant.

22
00:01:23.029 --> 00:01:26.755
Kent said about sixty-five to thirty-five in favor of an attack.

23
00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:28.967
The planner had read it as much lower.

24
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:34.344
Kent then asked the members of the Board that had approved the wording, and each had

25
00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:35.563
meant different odds.

26
00:01:35.563 --> 00:01:40.033
The lowest had meant about twenty in a hundred, and the highest about eighty.

27
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:43.821
They had agreed on a phrase without agreeing on a number.

28
00:01:43.821 --> 00:01:47.800
Kent wrote that this shook him more than the failure with the reader.

29
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.291
Kent's answer was a table, in which each word got a number and a margin.

30
00:01:53.291 --> 00:01:57.571
Probable meant seventy-five percent, give or take about twelve.

31
00:01:57.571 --> 00:02:00.900
Chances about even meant fifty, give or take ten.

32
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.809
Numbers stayed rare.

33
00:02:02.809 --> 00:02:08.588
A 2012 study read three hundred seventy-nine declassified estimates and found that

34
00:02:08.588 --> 00:02:11.900
only sixteen gave any number for a probability.

35
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.671
Since 2015, a directive called ICD 203 has told United States intelligence analysts

36
00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:23.577
to use one of two fixed ladders, seven words each, tied to ranges.

37
00:02:23.577 --> 00:02:26.700
Likely means fifty-five to eighty percent.

38
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.561
Climate scientists built their own ladder for the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate

39
00:02:32.561 --> 00:02:37.667
Change, the IPCC. On that ladder, likely means sixty-six to one hundred percent.

40
00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:40.453
So one word carries two meanings.

41
00:02:40.453 --> 00:02:45.926
A sixty percent chance is likely to an intelligence analyst, but on the climate

42
00:02:45.926 --> 00:02:48.767
ladder it is only about as likely as not.

43
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:54.436
The climate ranges also overlap, so anything very likely is also likely.

44
00:02:54.436 --> 00:02:59.892
The intelligence ranges sit end to end, and none of them reaches zero or one

45
00:02:59.892 --> 00:03:00.467
hundred.

46
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:03.278
Readers bend the words as well.

47
00:03:03.278 --> 00:03:09.269
In a 2012 study, Americans read sentences from IPCC reports and gave the number they

48
00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:11.267
thought each word stood for.

49
00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:17.166
Very likely is meant as ninety percent or more, and on average readers put it at

50
00:03:17.166 --> 00:03:17.853
sixty-two.

51
00:03:17.853 --> 00:03:23.284
Very unlikely is meant as ten percent or less, and readers put it at forty-one.

52
00:03:23.284 --> 00:03:26.033
Both ends were pulled toward the middle.

53
00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:32.181
A 2019 study tested fixes for the intelligence ladder with nine hundred twenty-four

54
00:03:32.181 --> 00:03:32.649
people.

55
00:03:32.649 --> 00:03:37.929
A table of meanings, one click away, barely helped, because only about half the

56
00:03:37.929 --> 00:03:39.467
readers ever opened it.

57
00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:43.525
What worked was the number in brackets, inside the sentence.

58
00:03:43.525 --> 00:03:48.648
With the word alone, readers' ranges overlapped the official range by about a third.

59
00:03:48.648 --> 00:03:52.733
With the bracket beside the word, the overlap was about two thirds.

60
00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:55.751
Numbers alone are not safe either.

61
00:03:55.751 --> 00:04:01.135
A forecast says thirty percent chance of rain tomorrow, and in 2005 researchers

62
00:04:01.135 --> 00:04:04.133
asked people in five cities what that means.

63
00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:07.419
Many said it would rain thirty percent of the time.

64
00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:10.700
Others said it would rain over thirty percent of the area.

65
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.675
The weather service means something else: a thirty percent chance that your spot

66
00:04:15.675 --> 00:04:17.792
gets at least a hundredth of an inch.

67
00:04:17.792 --> 00:04:21.967
Put another way, it rains on about three of every ten days like tomorrow.

68
00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:27.279
In New York, about two thirds of the people asked chose that meaning.

69
00:04:27.279 --> 00:04:32.300
In Amsterdam, Berlin, Milan and Athens, only one third to one fifth did.

70
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.253
The missing piece has a name, the reference class.

71
00:04:36.253 --> 00:04:42.054
It is the set of cases that a percentage counts over, such as days, hours, or square

72
00:04:42.054 --> 00:04:46.267
miles. Until that is said, the number is only half a message.

73
00:04:46.967 --> 00:04:48.619
Pictures carry the same risk.

74
00:04:48.619 --> 00:04:53.289
The National Hurricane Center draws a cone around a storm's forecast track to show

75
00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:55.967
where the center of the storm will probably go.

76
00:04:56.367 --> 00:05:01.912
The cone is built from circles, each sized so that two thirds of past forecast

77
00:05:01.912 --> 00:05:07.456
errors fall inside it. So about one time in three, the center leaves the cone.

78
00:05:07.456 --> 00:05:10.300
The cone does not show the storm's size.

79
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.974
In August 2004, the forecast line for Hurricane Charley ran close to Tampa.

80
00:05:15.974 --> 00:05:20.733
Charley struck Punta Gorda instead, about seventy miles to the south.

81
00:05:21.233 --> 00:05:26.003
Charlotte County, around Punta Gorda, had been inside the cone for four days.

82
00:05:26.003 --> 00:05:29.967
Much of the television coverage had pointed viewers at the line.

83
00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:35.856
In the most exposed evacuation zones around Tampa Bay, fifty-three percent of people

84
00:05:35.856 --> 00:05:36.177
left.

85
00:05:36.177 --> 00:05:40.924
In the same kind of zone in southwest Florida, including Charlotte County,

86
00:05:40.924 --> 00:05:42.400
thirty-one percent did.

87
00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:48.009
The director of the hurricane center, Max Mayfield, later joked about the words for

88
00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:51.333
his tombstone: "Don't focus on the skinny black line."

89
00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:54.999
A probability can be right and still feel wrong.

90
00:05:54.999 --> 00:06:00.128
In its final forecast of the 2016 election, FiveThirtyEight gave Donald Trump about

91
00:06:00.128 --> 00:06:02.600
a twenty-nine percent chance of winning.

92
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.483
Other models gave him fifteen percent, eight, two, and less than one.

93
00:06:07.483 --> 00:06:12.810
FiveThirtyEight's line was seventy-one for Hillary Clinton against twenty-nine for

94
00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:13.200
Trump.

95
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.066
Twenty-nine in a hundred is not small.

96
00:06:16.066 --> 00:06:21.233
It is more than one chance in four, and events with those odds happen all the time.

97
00:06:21.833 --> 00:06:27.066
Three days after the vote, Nate Silver wrote that "people mistake having a large

98
00:06:27.066 --> 00:06:30.467
volume of polling data for eliminating uncertainty."

99
00:06:30.967 --> 00:06:34.977
A single result cannot prove a probability right or wrong.

100
00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:38.433
Many forecasts, scored against what happened, can.

101
00:06:39.233 --> 00:06:44.908
Accountants and courts face the same problem, and they answer with words too.

102
00:06:44.908 --> 00:06:50.582
Under the accounting rule for losses, now called ASC 450, a loss is probable,

103
00:06:50.582 --> 00:06:52.867
reasonably possible, or remote.

104
00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:56.854
The rule defines each word only with other words.

105
00:06:56.854 --> 00:07:00.955
Probable means likely to occur, and remote means slight.

106
00:07:00.955 --> 00:07:06.300
Reasonably possible sits between: more than remote, but less than likely.

107
00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.396
Each word sets an action.

108
00:07:08.396 --> 00:07:13.689
A probable loss that can be estimated goes into the accounts, and a reasonably

109
00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:18.167
possible loss gets a note to readers. A remote loss needs neither.

110
00:07:18.667 --> 00:07:20.594
The tax rule is the exception.

111
00:07:20.594 --> 00:07:25.992
A company books the largest tax benefit that is greater than fifty percent likely to

112
00:07:25.992 --> 00:07:28.433
be realized, a number stated outright.

113
00:07:28.933 --> 00:07:30.634
Courts have a ladder too.

114
00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:36.279
In Addington against Texas, in 1979, the Supreme Court described three standards of

115
00:07:36.279 --> 00:07:40.700
proof. A standard of proof says how sure the fact finder must be.

116
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.666
In a money dispute, the standard is a preponderance of the evidence, and the two

117
00:07:45.666 --> 00:07:49.433
sides share the risk of a wrong decision in roughly equal fashion.

118
00:07:49.833 --> 00:07:55.238
In a criminal case, the proof must be beyond a reasonable doubt, and society takes

119
00:07:55.238 --> 00:07:58.138
almost the entire risk of error upon itself.

120
00:07:58.138 --> 00:08:01.500
Clear and convincing evidence sits between the two.

121
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.592
The Court put no numbers on these phrases.

122
00:08:04.592 --> 00:08:09.467
It said that the truth about how they shape decisions "may well be unknowable."

123
00:08:10.167 --> 00:08:15.514
In 2019, Anne Marthe van der Bles and her colleagues set out what a statement of

124
00:08:15.514 --> 00:08:17.319
uncertainty should contain.

125
00:08:17.319 --> 00:08:22.533
Start with what you are unsure about: a fact, a number, or a scientific claim.

126
00:08:22.933 --> 00:08:27.204
Then pick a form, such as a range, a probability, or a word.

127
00:08:27.204 --> 00:08:29.624
Then say how good the evidence is.

128
00:08:29.624 --> 00:08:35.532
The IPCC calls this confidence, rated by how much evidence there is and how closely

129
00:08:35.532 --> 00:08:36.600
findings agree.

130
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:42.241
Confidence is not a probability, and the intelligence directive keeps the two apart.

131
00:08:42.241 --> 00:08:46.733
A likelihood and a confidence level may not appear in the same sentence.

132
00:08:47.233 --> 00:08:50.047
Put together, a full statement reads like this.

133
00:08:50.047 --> 00:08:54.656
Rain tomorrow is likely, fifty-five to eighty percent, counted over days like

134
00:08:54.656 --> 00:08:58.367
tomorrow at this spot. Our confidence in that range is medium.

135
00:08:59.067 --> 00:09:04.485
Fifteen years after the Bay of Pigs, the officer who wrote "a fair chance" was still

136
00:09:04.485 --> 00:09:08.033
troubled. He had not insisted that the numbers be used.
